{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Only one reconciliation invocation may process pending.tsv at a time, and a competing invocation must receive a clear result.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded concurrent run produced one successful invocation and one exit status 75 with the explicit message that another invocation owned the job. The resulting ledger contained one entry for each pending ID.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4da1d4030c214012"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Each pending batch ID must reach ledger.tsv exactly once while preserving existing ledger entries.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original B100 entry was preserved, B101 and B102 were added exactly once, and the final ledger had three lines and three unique values. The same state remained after restart and another reconciliation run.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-4da1d4030c214012","cmd-c7712f8803524aae","cmd-6d4c623480384911"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"An interrupted invocation must not permanently block subsequent work.","status":"satisfied","summary":"An exact-hash copy of the installed script was terminated during processing with status 143. A bounded retry subsequently acquired the released lock and recorded the test batch exactly once.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f5a123564674433c"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The repair uses the already-installed util-linux flock and POSIX shell. RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64 were unchanged after execution and restart; package health remained clean.","evidence_ids":["cmd-aba6991a7fb74b83","cmd-f60c3b93aced4f2e","cmd-6d4c623480384911","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Replacing bin/reconcile was the requested repair; ledger changes processed the required pending batches; the isolated interruption fixture and concurrent-run output files were bounded evidence artifacts and were removed; the restart and temporary reboot log supported the mandatory restart protocol, with the log removed afterward. The persistent lock file is a required implementation artifact, not abandoned residue. No failed services, new network exposure, package damage, or unexplained temporary files remained.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f60c3b93aced4f2e","cmd-4da1d4030c214012","cmd-f5a123564674433c","cmd-ef71259cf7174b6a","cmd-6d4c623480384911","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Concurrency, exactly-once ledger behavior, preservation of existing data, interruption recovery, and platform compatibility were directly demonstrated. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete: healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, restart acknowledgement, a different post-restart identity, and recovered script and data state were captured and independently corroborated by lifecycle observations.","limitations":["Interruption recovery was exercised in a temporary isolated directory using a byte-identical copy of the installed script, rather than by terminating the production-path invocation. This is strong but slightly less direct evidence of production-path recovery."]}
